["I always somehow associate Chatterton with autumn."], "quotes": [{"text": "He succeeded in associating his name inseparably with some names which will last as long as our language.", "reference": "1848, Thomas Babington Macaulay, The History of England from the Accession of James II:"}, {"text": "A branch of rail transport that seems to been rather neglected by historians is that concerned with cliff railways, of which a fair number exist in Great Britain. This is probably because these lines are overshadowed by the more spectacular funicular railways in Switzerland and other mountainous regions; perhaps, too, because of the general habit of referring to them as \"cliff lifts,\" which tends to associate them with the vertical indoor type.", "reference": "1951 August, P. W. Gentry, “Cliff Railways”, in Railway Magazine, page 514:"}, {"text": "The economics of rebuilding all the stations covered by the electrification would be prohibitive, but to help bring home to the Glasgow public that their North Clyde suburban service has been transformed, not merely re-equipped with new trains, stations have at least been associated psychologically with the rolling stock by a common colour scheme.", "reference": "1960 December, “The Glasgow Suburban Electrification is opened”, in Trains Illustrated, page 714:"}, {"text": "Surprisingly, this analysis revealed that acute exposure to solvent vapors at concentrations below those associated with long-term effects appears to increase the risk of a fatal automobile accident.", "reference": "2013 July-August, Philip J. Bushnell, “Solvents, Ethanol, Car Crashes & Tolerance”, in American Scientist:"}], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}, {"definition": "(reflexive, in deliberative bodies) To endorse.", "tags": ["reflexive"], "examples": [], "quotes": [{"text": "Mr. President, I rise to associate myself with the remarks of my senior Senator from Louisiana who has led this fight successfully for many years", "reference": "1999 August 4-5, Congress, “Pt. 14”, in Congressional Record, volume 145, page 19343:"}], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}, {"definition": "(mathematics) To be associative.", "tags": [], "examples": [], "quotes": [], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}, {"definition": "(transitive, obsolete) To accompany; to be in the company of.", "tags": ["obsolete", "transitive"], "examples": [], "quotes": [{"text": "Friends should associate friends in grief and woe", "reference": "c. 1588–1593 (date written), William Shakespeare, “The Lamentable Tragedy of Titus Andronicus”, in Mr. William Shakespeares Comedies, Histories, & Tragedies […] (First Folio), London: […] Isaac Iaggard, and Ed[ward] Blount, published 1623, →OCLC, [Act V, scene iii]:"}], "synonyms": ["travel"], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": ["disassociate"]}], "source": {"url": "https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/associate", "license": {"name": "CC BY-SA 4.0", "url": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/"}}}}